{
  "result": "When the last patron slips through the revolving door and the security guard dims the foyer lights, the library exhales, like a whale resurfacing after a day of hosting barnacled traffic. The hush that follows is not silence but rather a rearrangement of sounds: the faint whir of climate\u2010control vents, the creak of century\u2010old floorboards settling into their evening stretch, and the clandestine tick of a distant clock whose face no longer sees daylight customers. Under the subdued glow of exit signs, books begin their nocturnal murmuring. Hardcover biographies brag about triumphant chapters left open on reading tables, while slim volumes of poetry practice vowels, trying on long-forgotten pronunciations. Even atlases, those bulky cartographic monarchs, shift their pages as though tectonic plates underneath their printed continents still felt restless.\n\nSomewhere in the children\u2019s wing, a paper dragon illustrated in jagged greens coils off a page, testing its joints, rehearsing smoke rings that will dissolve by dawn. A moth patrols the stacks, occasionally landing on the spine of a thriller, conferring with the embossed title as if checking references. Upstairs, the reference section glints with the subdued authority of encyclopedias holding centuries inside their stitched bellies; they radiate a confidence unavailable to the internet\u2019s skittish hyperlinks.\n\nBy three a.m., the library\u2019s population of thoughts, footnotes, and half-remembered lines reaches its nightly summit, an invisible crowd humming in polyglot harmony. And then, as dawn nudges the curtains, everything retreats, returning to stillness before keys rattle in the lock, and welcome visitors again.",
